+#### Impact, blocks and page changes
+
+Impact uses `impact-1.0.0` with `basis: deterministic_rule` and
+`language_profile: de-en`. Every change and table change carries a level,
+priority (`high`, `normal` or `low`), `kinds` and `signals`. The text
+classifier prefers normalized source text, with raw text as fallback.
+
+| Level | Rule |
+| --- | --- |
+| `non_content` | Identical text or equal glyph folds. Deterministic artifact and structural-only routes also use this low-priority level. |
+| `editorial` | The tokens agree after editorial folding. Records have low priority and stay visible with their existing resolution. |
+| `content` | A one-sided finding or a paired lexical difference. A paired finding is high priority when changed tokens carry a material signal. An isolated one-sided text finding is high only with **at most 40 word tokens** and at least one of `modal`, `negation`, `condition`, `number`, `date` or `reference`. Other content findings have normal priority. |
+| `moved` | Separate relative-order or section-change record, with move kinds and priorities from Stage 9. |
+
+Glyph folding composes Unicode, applies per-character compatibility folding
+while preserving number glyphs such as superscripts, removes invisible
+format characters, canonicalizes selected quotes/dashes and collapses spaces.
+It removes leading decorative bullet glyphs and can remove a spaced leading
+ASCII hyphen when no quantity follows. State marks stay distinct. Stage 11's
+raw signature guard protects digits, signs and state marks even when folds agree.
+Glyph kinds include `unicode_composition`, `ligature_or_compatibility_form`,
+`invisible_character`, `quote_or_dash_variant`, `whitespace` and `bullet_glyph`.
+
+Editorial folding handles letter case, selected punctuation and gender forms,
+German numeric formats, supported number words and `Prozent`/`%`. It keeps
+dates, ambiguous number formats and numeric operators between quantities.
+It joins a hyphen plus space before a lowercase continuation, excluding
+suspended hyphens such as `Ein- und Ausgang` (`hyphenation`). It expands a
+fixed abbreviation list such as `z. B.`, `d. h.` and `Abs.`, with
+`abbreviation` recorded only when usage counts differ. If both sides have
+recognized leading enumeration labels such as `a)`, `(b)`, `iv)` or `3)`,
+it compares the remainder and records a different label as `list_label`.
+The label rule leaves a leading `1.` in the text because it can mark the day
+in a date. German `ß` folds to `ss` only at word end or before a consonant. Other kinds include `letter_case`,
+`number_format`, `orthography`, `gender_form` and `punctuation`.
+
+Changed token spans can signal `modal`, `negation`, `qualifier`, `condition`,
+`number`, `date`, `reference` or `state_marker`. Operations also record
+`added_text`, `deleted_text` and `replaced_text`. A modified element with a
+changed source type adds `element_type_changed`. Table changes take the
+strongest changed-cell impact, or `content` with `table_structure_changed`
+for structural changes. Table structure has normal priority unless a changed
+cell carries high priority. These rules describe impact independently of
+matching confidence and review outcome.
+
+A `BLOCK-…` record groups at least **three consecutive** `DELETED` changes
+in OLD or `ADDED` changes in NEW. Skip layout and eligible furniture. Any
+other element outside the run, including a surviving heading or table, ends it.
+A changed heading can belong to the run. Each member keeps its `change_id` and
+gets `block_id`, normal priority and the `block_member` kind. The block
+records member IDs in reading order, physical pages, the preceding heading and
+`contains_heading`. It has normal content impact and the union of member
+signals, with kind `section_added`/`section_deleted` when it includes a
+heading and `block_added`/`block_deleted` otherwise.
+
+The queue collapses a block's member reviews to one `source_kind: change_block`
+entry with their IDs and combined reasons. Residual proposal groups can join
+that entry when all their members are in the block and they have no counterpart
+proposals. The block review keeps those `proposal_group_ids`. Groups with
+counterpart proposals or members outside one block retain their own reviews.
+
+A physical page qualifies if it has at least one counted element and every
+non-layout element on that page has the appropriate one-sided change
+(`DELETED` in OLD or `ADDED` in NEW). This includes TOC elements and requires
+any physical table fragment to belong to a same-direction one-sided table
+change. A surviving or modified element or table prevents qualification.
+Stage 14 groups consecutive qualifying pages by side into `PAGE-…` records.
+Each records physical pages, member change/table/block IDs, element count,
+a preceding heading and an approximate opposite-side anchor page. The latter
+is the counterpart's physical page for the nearest preceding paired element,
+using Stage 6 exact matches and Stage 9 modified, modified-review and
+duplicate-exact-review pairs. Pairs in `moves` are excluded. Either anchor
+can be absent. Page impact is normal content (`page_added`/`page_deleted`).
+
+A member change carries `page_change_id`, as does a table change linked to
+just one page group. A block's `full_pages` lists the qualifying pages it
+touches. The block gets `page_change_id` only if all its members belong to
+the same page group. The reviewer displays whole-page labels through blocks
+and still exposes their member changes. See the
+[matching contract](contracts/phase1-matching.md#whole-page-changes) for
+the record shape.
+
+Statistics include `moves`, `change_blocks`, `changes_in_blocks` and
+`page_changes`, plus `impact_level_counts` and `impact_priority_counts` over
+`changes` and `table_impact_level_counts` over `table_changes`. The page
+count here is the number of group records. These fields are additive.
